  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1130 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late Friday \nevening reveal a strong longwave trough along and west of the \nRockies, with a high-amplitude ridge downstream extending from the \nArkLaTex region northward through the upper Midwest. At the surface, \nsouthwest KS resides between broad high pressure over much of the \neastern CONUS and a ~997-mb low reaching from its center in south-\ncentral Canada southward into the central plains. Through the \nremainder of the overnight period, a secondary, weaker lee trough \nwill develop in eastern CO/far western KS, which should result in \nweakening southerly winds. However, this will not occur until near \nsunrise, allowing winds to remain elevated much of the night and \nholding Saturday morning lows in the upper 50s/near 60. Daytime \nSaturday, the aforementioned strong upper low will inch eastward \ntowards the Four Corners region. As DPVA increases over the central \nplains, widespread showers and thunderstorms will form in eastern CO \nand propagate into western KS by late afternoon/early evening. High \nresolution guidance suggests this activity will remain limited to \nareas along and west of US-83, and will pose no severe threat owing \nto negligible instability. Precipitation will gradually taper off \novernight, with only scattered light rain showers by Sunday morning.\n\nDaytime Sunday, some disagreement between the HREF members and the \nglobal ensembles emerge as the core of the low pressure system aloft \nadvances towards the central Rockies. HREF members indicate training \nshowers with a few thunderstorms will move south-southwest to north-\nnortheast through much of the day Sunday, but this is in contrast to \nboth the ECMWF ENS and GEFS which confine the bulk of the \nprecipitation again to areas along and west of US-83. Given the \ntrend over the last couple days in the global ensembles has been a \nslower, farther west solution with the largest precipitation \namounts, this scenario will be favored. \n\nMedium range ensembles suggest precipitation may linger into Monday \nand perhaps even Tuesday as the upper low takes its sweet time \nejecting onto the plains, and a secondary shortwave trough moves \nacross the northern plains. Ensemble total precipitation fields once \nall is said and done range from 0.5-1.5\" along and west of a \nWakeeney to Liberal line, with locally higher amounts to 2\" \npossible, and 0.1-0.5\" to the east of this line. The remainder of \nthe forecast period will feature much more seasonable \ntemperatures with highs in the 60s and low 70s, and little to no\nprecipitation chances until the end of the business week.",
